 \\).\n\nGiven the polynomial \\( f(x) = kx^4 + 9x^3 + kx^2 + 32x - 11 \\), we need to find the value of \\( k \\) such that the remainder when \\( f(x) \\) is divided by \\( x + 5 \\) is 4. According to the Remainder Theorem, this means \\( f(-5) = 4 \\).\n\nFirst, we substitute \\( x = -5 \\) into the polynomial \\( f(x) \\):\n\n\\[\nf(-5) = k(-5)^4 + 9(-5)^3 + k(-5)^2 + 32(-5) - 11\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate each term individually:\n\n\\[\n(-5)^4 = 625\n\\]\n\\[\n9(-5)^3 = 9(-125) = -1125\n\\]\n\\[\nk(-5)^2 = k(25) = 25k\n\\]\n\\[\n32(-5) = -160\n\\]\n\nSubstituting these values back into the polynomial, we get:\n\n\\[\nf(-5) = k(625) + (-1125) + 25k + (-160) - 11\n\\]\n\nCombine the like terms:\n\n\\[\nf(-5) = 625k - 1125 + 25k - 160 - 11\n\\]\n\\[\nf(-5) = 650k - 1306\n\\]\n\nWe know from the problem statement that \\( f(-5) = 4 \\). Therefore, we set up the equation:\n\n\\[\n650k - 1306 = 4\n\\]\n\nSolve for \\( k \\):\n\n\\[\n650k = 4 + 1306\n\\]\n\\[\n650k = 1310\n\\]\n\\[\nk = \\frac{1310}{650}\n\\]\n\\[\nk = \\frac{131}{65}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( k \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{131}{65}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{131}{65}}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"intermediate_algebra:583"}
